{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim contradicts its own proposed evidence-distinguishing test. The problem asks what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. The target's mechanism says the reliability gain comes from error cancellation via the crowd, not from checklist compliance — but its two counterconditions (groupthink/social loafing, and systemic checklist errors causing uniform failure) are both crowd-dynamics failure modes, not tests that separate genuine reliability from coincidental checklist-tracking. The 'systemic checklist errors' countercondition only shows when the panel FAILS; it provides no single-evaluation evidence criterion for identifying when the panel's SUCCESS reflects genuine error-cancellation rather than the checklist merely happening to encode the right answer. A panel could produce the correct verdict purely because the checklist's fixed steps happen to match the task's correct path, with zero independent error-cancellation occurring among panelists. The target never specifies what observable within one evaluation (e.g., pattern of disagreement-and-correction, variance across panelists on sub-items) would distinguish that coincidental case from genuine crowd-driven gain. By attributing the gain to crowd aggregation while offering only crowd-failure counterconditions, it leaves the core distinguishing-evidence requirement unmet.","counterexample":[{"setup":"A panel of non-experts uses a checklist that, unbeknownst to anyone, contains steps perfectly aligned with the one correct solution path for a specific task. All panelists follow the checklist mechanically and arrive at the correct answer with near-zero disagreement and no instance of one panelist catching another's error.","question":"Does this single evaluation distinguish a genuine reliability gain via error cancellation from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer?"}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}